<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thurgau</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Canton of Thurgau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thurgau is a region and canton of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, lying between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thur">Thur</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frauenfeld">Frauenfeld</a>. Once a large Alemannic district, it was held largely by the counts of Kyburg and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> until the Swiss Confederates conquered it in 1460. Ruled as a common lordship of the cantons, it became a full canton in 1803.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thurgau.jpg" alt="Thurgau" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tschubby · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karte_Kanton_Thurgau_2010.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
